cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use these materials, please cite them as below."
type: dataset
title: "anime-materials"
authors:
  - name: "weft"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/weftspun/anny-render-corpus"
abstract: >-
  Procedurally generated MToon material parameters for anime-styled characters, stratified
  across the ten Monk Skin Tone levels. The corpus is the parameter layer plus a seed, not a
  set of pixels: anime_materials.usda carries the tone identities and the sampled axes, and
  check_anime_materials.py re-derives everything that follows from them.

  Constructed rather than generated. No generative model produces any part of this, so the
  same seed reproduces the corpus and every label is true by construction.

  THE SHADE COLOUR IS WHERE TONE EQUITY IS DECIDED. MToon extends glTF pbrMetallicRoughness
  rather than replacing it, so the lit colour is baseColorFactor and the extension adds a
  shade colour that PBR does not constrain. A single shade multiplier is not equitable: at
  x0.6 the lit-to-shade separation is dE 17.3 at MST 1 and dE 4.8 at MST 10, so the cel
  terminator that defines the form on a light character is 3.6x weaker on a dark one. The
  multiplier is solved per tone to hold contrast constant instead.

  APPLYING THE MONK SCALE TO ANIME IS A SUBSTITUTION AND IS RECORDED AS ONE. The scale is
  defined on photographs of real skin. Anime skin is an artistic choice rather than a
  reflectance, and no anime skin tone standard exists to use instead: Danbooru's tags are a
  folksonomy with no colour values, defined relative to "the usual Eurasian skin tone", and
  its own forum records canonically dark-skinned characters being redrawn as tans. So these
  cells give coverage of a perceptual range. They do not claim any cell depicts a real
  population, and characters with non-human skin have no Monk code at all.
references:
  - type: standard
    title: "VRMC_materials_mtoon-1.0"
    authors:
      - name: "VRM Consortium"
    url: "https://github.com/vrm-c/vrm-specification/tree/master/specification/VRMC_materials_mtoon-1.0"
    notes: >-
      The material model. shadeColorFactor and shadingToonyFactor are the two parameters the
      equity argument turns on; shading is linearstep(-1 + toony, 1 - toony, shading).
  - type: standard
    title: "Monk Skin Tone Scale"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Monk"
        given-names: "Ellis"
    url: "https://skintone.google/"
    notes: >-
      Ten levels. CIELAB at D65 is authoritative and the sRGB hex is the published conversion
      from it. This layer carries the hex, which is a known substitution to replace with the
      CIELAB triple.
  - type: generic
    title: "Tag Group: Skin Color"
    authors:
      - name: "Danbooru"
    url: "https://danbooru.donmai.us/wiki_pages/tag_group:skin_color"
    notes: >-
      Cited for the absence it establishes: pale, fair, light, tan, dark, very dark and black
      skin are community tags without colour values, so they are not a scale to measure on.
  - type: report
    title: "Identity in Metaverse: Social VR Lifestyle Survey Deep-dive"
    authors:
      - name: "Nem"
      - family-names: "Bredikhina"
        given-names: "Liudmila"
    year: 2024
    url: "https://note.com/nemchan_nel/"
    notes: >-
      n=1,012, fielded 24 June to 12 July 2024, supervised by Dr. Junji Ohyama. Cited for the
      finding that 66 percent of respondents prefer an avatar unlike their physical selves,
      which is why the tone sweep is uniform rather than weighted to any population. The
      survey measures no skin tone and no ethnicity, and its sample is 73.3 percent Japan.
